{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790971673,"nonce":"bemrb2t9i7540mIne0FL5JOY910=","title":"Hot reload accepts an edit that adds a closure, then crashes the program","message":"A reload whose only change is one more closure in a method (a new function,\nso later findexes shift) is accepted on the --hot-reload LLVM path: ash\nrecompiles every shifted function, about 300 here, which takes about 45 s,\nand the program then crashes with SIGSEGV in HLInterpreter::op_field_get on\nits next call.\n\nReproducer: in the ashui repo, `sh tests/fixtures/hotreload/run.sh v3`.\n- It builds Main with v1/Panel.hx and runs it under\n  `ash --mode hybrid --hot-reload`. Main polls hl.Api.checkReload() every\n  20 ms.\n- It then rebuilds the same .hl with v3/Panel.hx, which adds one reactive\n  attribute to a template. That reactive attribute is one more closure,\n  `() -\u003e (count * 2 : Single)`.\n- Run it from ashui's root; it needs cargo, haxe with tink_hxx, and this\n  checkout at ../ash.\n\nOutput: `[hot-reload] reloaded 300 changed function(s)`, then\n`=== CRASH: SIGSEGV ... op_field_get`. Body-only edits in the same fixture\n(v2, v4) reload correctly and keep state.\n\nTwo plain-Haxe attempts to reproduce it without ashui were refused cleanly\ninstead. They are in ashui's tests/fixtures/ash/reload_added_function:\n- an added method gives \"type field layout changed\";\n- an added closure gives \"global count changed (91 -\u003e 92)\".\nSo ashui's case gets past both checks. By code reading, the stricter\nrefusal() (functions added or removed, a findex now naming a different\nfunction; reload.rs:570-601) runs in stage_reload and the non-LLVM do_reload,\nbut not in perform_reload, which hl.Api.checkReload uses on LLVM builds.\n\nExpected: refuse the reload with a message, as the other two cases do,\nrather than accept it. Longer term, UI hot reload needs this kind of edit to\nwork. A template's reactive expressions and handlers are closures, so adding\none is the most common template edit. That would mean matching functions by\nidentity (class, method, closure ordinal) rather than findex and compiling\nthe added ones.\n\nAlso worth noting: a two-line body edit (v2) reports 72-98 changed functions.","files":null}]}